  "input": "以下の記事から、英語上級者が学ぶ価値のある単語・フレーズ・表現・イディオムを5つ選んでください。\n\n**以下の順序で処理し、上位条件を優先してください。**\n\n1. **本文一致（絶対条件）**：本文中の連続した語句だけを抜き出す。変更・言い換え・追加・創作は禁止。\n2. **5語以内（絶対条件）**：スペース区切りで正確に数える。6語以上は出力禁止。\n3. **学習ポイントの明確化と最小化（必須）**：学ぶべき語彙・熟語・表現を明確にし、意味と自然さを保てる最短の形で抽出する。不要な目的語・修飾語・文脈は除く。5語は上限であり、目標ではない。短縮によって学習価値が失われる場合のみ、必要な語を残す。\n4. **上級者向けの学習価値**：上級者でも学ぶ価値のある語彙・イディオム・慣用表現を優先する。\n5. **易しい表現の除外**：基本語だけで構成され、字面どおりに意味が分かる表現や、上級者なら知っている易しい表現は除外する。\n6. **5件選定**：条件を満たす候補から5件選ぶ。適切な候補が不足する場合のみ5件未満とし、数合わせはしない。\n\n**出力直前に各候補を本文と照合し、改変がないこと、5語以内であることを再確認してください。語数も併記してください。**\n\n---\n\n【記事本文】\n# If You Had to Watch a Stone for Five Minutes, Would Your “Bad-Guy Switch” Turn On?\n\nA stone. Still a stone. Surely something will happen soon—no, it’s a stone. Nothing happens on the screen. That goes on for five minutes. But you, the viewer, can still get one thing done: cut someone else’s reward.\n\nThis sounds grim all of a sudden, but it’s about an online experiment with participants in the United States. They were randomly assigned to watch either a five-minute video of a stone, where nothing happened, or a video of a magician.\n\nDuring that time, they had three choices: reduce another participant’s reward, increase it, or do nothing. They could change it by 10 cents at a time, up to a maximum of 50 cents. The stone on the screen stayed silent, while the viewers were handed “a little power.”\n\nSo, did boredom become fuel for meanness?\n\nParticipants who were strongly inclined to enjoy seeing others suffer were more likely to reduce the other person’s reward after watching the stone video than after watching the magician video. Among those with a weaker tendency, there was no clear difference.\n\nYou might want to dismiss this by saying, “So it really is a matter of personality.” But there’s more to the story.\n\nIn the next experiment, participants were again randomly assigned to groups. But this time, there was no option to increase the reward. The only choice was to reduce it. This time, regardless of how strongly participants were inclined to enjoy seeing others suffer, those who watched the stone video were more likely to reduce another person’s reward than those in the other conditions.\n\nWell, if we focus only on personality, we may miss something. Along with “what kind of person someone is,” there’s also “what they can do in that situation.” We thought we were reading a profile of the person, but now we start noticing the stage setup, too.\n\nBut it’s too soon to jump to “Add a button to increase rewards and bring world peace!” The two were separate experiments. We can’t say that the difference in choices alone caused the difference in results. We don’t know that offering an “increase” option can prevent meanness.\n\nThe experiment also dealt only with brief boredom and actions that reduced another person’s reward. We can’t apply its findings directly to everyday meanness or boredom that lasts a long time.\n\nEven so, our view of boring moments shifts a little. We can ask not only “What mustn’t I do?” but also “What else can I do instead?” Before looking into someone’s personality, look at the menu in front of us. The stone may not move for five minutes, but the question we ask shifts a little.\n\n## In one line\nStone-watchers were more likely to cut another person’s reward—only those high in sadistic tendencies in one experiment, but people at every level in another. The findings suggest that the choices available in a situation may matter alongside personality. But these separate, brief experiments do not show that offering an increase option prevents meanness or that the findings apply to everyday life.",
